{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792301921,"nonce":"G4XFhIrPDair6FvugDmIGmGXVFA=","title":"CFF (PostScript-outline) glyphs rasterize in font units, a thousand pixels a side","message":"`GlyphRasterizer::rasterize_at_offset` and its colour variant build a swash scaler at the requested size with swash's default `hint(false)`. For a face with CFF outlines (an `OTTO` OpenType font such as macOS's SF Mono) swash then rasterizes the outline in font units, not pixels. Measured here with SF-Mono-Regular.otf, glyph '3' at 32px: 992x1503 unhinted, 18x25 hinted. Each such glyph takes a huge atlas rect, the coverage atlas fills after a few of them, and every glyph after it is lost. TrueType outlines scale correctly either way.\n\nReported by the ashui session (its git-bug c2cfa79), whose default mono stack resolves to SF Mono.\n\nFix: hint the scaler when the face has a `CFF ` or `CFF2` table (checked per face through swash's table lookup, so collections work), in both functions. TrueType stays unhinted.\n\nTests: tests/cff_rasterize.rs. The TrueType case runs everywhere (bundled Arial, a 32px glyph is under 64px). The CFF case uses SF Mono and is skipped where the machine has no CFF font, since no open-licence CFF font is committed; adding one (any OTTO font under an open licence) would make it run on CI. It fails without the fix (1503px).","files":null}]}